                <header class="mb-8 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
                    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-2">WikiHumMCP</h1>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-500">How to configure access to WikiHumMCP</p>
                </header>

                <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

                    <p>WikiHumMCP gives language model applications such as ChatGPT and Claude access to data from the historical <a href="https://wikihum.lab.dariah.pl/">WikiHum</a> knowledge base. Through the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an application can search the database, retrieve information about its records, and make the queries needed to answer a question.</p>

                    <p>You can use the service through a simple online chat: <a href="https://ai.ihpan.edu.pl/wikihumchat/">WikiHumChat</a>.
                       You can also configure access in one of the popular tools, following the instructions below.
                    </p>

                    <p>Once the connection is configured, you can ask questions about WikiHum data in natural language. The scope and accuracy of the answers depend on the database contents and the model used. Check the information against the relevant WikiHum records and the sources they cite.</p>
                    <div class="note"><p><strong>Server address:</strong> <code>https://wb-mcp.wmcloud.org/wikihum/mcp</code><br><strong>Authentication:</strong> not required</p></div>
                    <p>The instructions below cover three applications. Option names and locations may change with updates; integration availability may also depend on your account type and organization settings.</p>
                    <nav class="wikihummcp-toc" aria-label="Contents">
                    <p>Set up the connection in:</p>
                    <ul><li><a href="#jan">Jan</a></li><li><a href="#claude">Claude</a></li><li><a href="#chatgpt">ChatGPT</a></li></ul>
                    </nav>
                    <section id="jan">

                    <h2>Jan</h2>
                    <p><a href="https://jan.ai/">Jan</a> is a desktop application for Windows, macOS, and Linux. It supports both local models and models provided by external services. To use WikiHumMCP, configure a model that supports tool calling; depending on the provider, you may need to enable this feature in the model settings.</p>

                    <h3>Add the server</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Open the application settings and go to <strong>MCP Servers</strong> (in some versions, this is under <strong>Integrations</strong>).</li>
                        <li>Select <strong>+ Add MCP Server</strong> and enter the following details:
                        <ul><li><strong>Name:</strong> <code>WikiHumMCP</code></li><li><strong>Transport:</strong> <code>HTTP</code></li><li><strong>URL:</strong> <code>https://wb-mcp.wmcloud.org/wikihum/mcp</code></li><li><strong>Timeout:</strong> 30 seconds; increase it if needed.</li></ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>Save the configuration and enable the server. A green indicator next to its name shows that the connection is active.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <div class="note"><p>Enter the address <strong>without a trailing slash</strong>. The server requires neither an API key nor a sign-in.</p></div>
                    <p>You can enable <strong>Allow All MCP Tool Permissions</strong> in the settings to approve MCP tool use automatically in all conversations. This setting is optional; without it, the application may ask for permission before using a tool.</p>

                    <h3>Test the connection</h3>
                    <p>Start a new conversation and select a model that supports tools. Use the tools icon beside the message field to check that the WikiHumMCP functions are available. To limit the search to WikiHum, you can turn off <strong>Web Search</strong>. Then enter, for example:</p>
                    <p><em>Search WikiHum for Jan Kostka and provide a link to the relevant record.</em></p>

                    <h3>Example of use</h3>
                    <p>The screenshots below show Jan using WikiHumMCP to answer the question, “Which artists associated with Kraków lived in the 16th–18th centuries?” The conversation shown is in Polish. Select an image to read it at full resolution.</p>
