                    <section id="claude">

                    <h2>Claude</h2>
                    <p><a href="https://claude.ai/">Claude</a> lets you add a remote MCP server as a custom connector. These instructions apply to the Claude application; connector availability and management depend on the account type and organization permissions. The Free plan allows one custom connector.</p>

                    <h3>Add the connector</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li>In the menu, select <strong>Customize → Connectors → + Add → Add custom connector</strong>.</li>
                        <li>Enter <code>WikiHumMCP</code> as the name and <code>https://wb-mcp.wmcloud.org/wikihum/mcp</code> as the address.</li>
                        <li>Complete the remaining setup steps. If asked how to authenticate, select <strong>No sign in</strong>; this server does not require a sign-in.</li>
                        <li>Add the connector and check that it is connected. Under <strong>Tool permissions</strong>, you can choose whether to allow each use of a tool individually or grant ongoing permission.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>Test the connection</h3>
                    <p>In a new conversation, enable the WikiHumMCP connector using the <strong>+</strong> button and the <strong>Connectors</strong> menu. Then enter:</p>
                    <p><em>Search WikiHum for Jan Kostka and provide a link to the relevant record.</em></p>
                    </section>
                    <section id="chatgpt">

                    <h2>ChatGPT</h2>
                    <p>In ChatGPT, you can add the WikiHumMCP server as a custom plugin. The ability to add and use custom MCP servers depends on your account type, the available interface, and workspace permissions. Configure it in the browser version of ChatGPT.</p>

                    <h3>Add the server</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Open <strong>Plugins</strong> in the sidebar, select <strong>+</strong>, and then <strong>Add custom MCP server</strong>.</li>
                        <li>Enter <code>WikiHumMCP</code> as the name and, optionally, add a description. Enter <code>https://wb-mcp.wmcloud.org/wikihum/mcp</code> in <strong>Server URL</strong>.</li>
                        <li>Under <strong>Authentication</strong>, select <strong>No authentication</strong>.</li>
                        <li>Read the connection notice, select <strong>I understand and want to continue</strong>, and choose <strong>Create as a plugin</strong>. Review the detected tools, then install the new plugin.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>Test the connection</h3>
                    <p>Start a new conversation in <strong>Work</strong> mode. Type <strong>@</strong> in the message field and select WikiHumMCP. Then ask, for example:</p>
                    <p><em>Search WikiHum for Jan Kostka and provide a link to the relevant record.</em></p>
                    <p>If the application asks for permission to use a tool, approve it to allow the data search.</p>
                    </section>
                    <section>

                    <h2>Connection problems</h2>
                    <p>If the server does not work, check the address, your internet connection, and whether the integration is enabled in the current conversation. In Jan, also make sure the address has no trailing slash and that the selected model supports tools. If the option to add a connector or plugin is missing, check your account restrictions or contact your workspace administrator.</p>
                    <p>For current setup instructions, consult the documentation for <a href="https://docs.jan.ai/desktop/integrations/mcp-servers">Jan</a>, <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166-get-started-with-custom-connectors-using-remote-mcp">Claude</a>, and <a href="https://developers.openai.com/plugins/quickstart">OpenAI</a>.</p>
                    </section>
